# Critiquing [Last Day On Earth Narrative](https://lacuna.org.uk/environment/climate-crisis-affecting-the-worlds-last-nomadic-afar-tribe-in-ethiopia/)
<p style="color: black; font-size:14px;"</p>
  The written format of <i>Last Day on Earth: How the climate crisis is affecting the world’s last nomadic Afar tribe, in Ethiopia</i> impacts how the story is told because it provides the author the ability to combine their personal experiences and thoughts with additional information about the environmental impacts that are affecting the Afar nomadic indigenous people of East Africa and share about their culture. Rather than just mentioning the environmental injustices that are occurring with statistics and organizational jargon, the author Soukaina Rabii interviewed individuals from the community, providing them with the platform to share their experiences and how they are managing environmental injustices at hand. This adds a complex layer of thought and meaning to the narrative, as we are hearing about what is happening from the people who are experiencing it in their own words. This allows the reader to gain a fuller view of what is happening and how the people affected are adapting to the changing environment. For the reader, this makes the environmental issues feel personal, relatable, and pressing rather than a distant problem in a place on the other side of the world. By having these embedded throughout the narrative, it draws in the reader and provides them with the ability to engage with the written words in a way that is not attainable by just hearing the authors' thoughts on the matter.
<p style="color: black; font-size:14px;"</p>
 When reading the interviews with Tamrat, Solal, Biruk, and Riwan and taking a step back to visualize what it would be like to firsthand experience changes in rainfall, migration, and traditional practices, it creates a sense of urgency for the reader to make positive changes in their lives for the benefit of all.[^1] For example, Solal described the relationship between environmental changes and identity, saying that he doesn't “recognize myself in the world anymore”; passages like this really add emphasis to the narrative and make the reader question if they can recognise themselves in the world they live in.[^1] The use of images alongside text provides the reader with visuals to connect back to the text and allows the reader to see how and what is happening, furthering the impact and emphasis of the text. I found the written format engaging because it changes its methods of delivery from description, statistics, historical context, and personal experiences. I like the use of section headings because it divides up the story into multiple parts. Although some may find it overwhelming that so many issues are addressed in one piece of work, I prefer the broad scope because it promotes more thought and gives readers more to relate to while showing how these issues are all connected.


