<p style="color: black; font-size:14px;"</p>
  ​The format of this story connects to both Thomas King's <i>Stories Can Be Both Wondrous and Dangerous</i> and Pixar in a Box’s <i>The Art of Storytelling</i>. King discusses how stories have the ability to shape how people understand the world and the people within it.[^2] Last Day on Earth, a written photo essay, demonstrates this by telling the climate crisis through the experiences of individual people from the Afar community rather than relying solely on environmental statistics, observations, and historical knowledge. Like King, Pixar In a Box discusses how important the organization of information is and how it affects the audience's engagement and emotional response.[^3] Last Day on Earth emulates this method of organizing information by introducing people and their everyday lives before expanding into larger issues such as drought, food insecurity, migration, and cultural change. This provides the reader with the background information and a general understanding of what is happening before diving deeper into the environmental issues at hand and how they're affecting the Afar nomadic indigenous people of East Africa.

